{
  "analysis": "This is an interesting trail of process creation events on WS-05.corp.example.\nLet's break them down:\n1. `evt-00305` and `evt-00351`: `whoami.exe /groups` run by CORP\\m.ward under Powershell.\n2. `evt-00437`: `fodhelper.exe` run by CORP\\m.ward under Powershell (UAC bypass attempt?).\n3. `evt-00445`: `svchost.exe -k netsvcs -p -s Appinfo` run by SYSTEM.\n4. `evt-00478`: `consent.exe` (UAC prompt consent) run by SYSTEM.\n5. `evt-00858`: `fodhelper.exe` run again.\n6. `evt-01186`: `powershell.exe -NoP -NonI -W Hidden -c $x=$((gp HKCU:Software\Microsoft\Windows Update).Update); powershell -NoP -NonI -W Hidden -enc $x` run by CORP\\m.ward with `fodhelper.exe` as parent. This is a classic UAC bypass technique using `fodhelper` registry hijack (writing to `HKCU:\\Software\\Classes\\ms-settings\\shell\\open\\command`, or similar, wait, here it reads from `HKCU:Software\\Microsoft\\Windows Update` property `Update` and executes it as base64encoded payload).\n7. `evt-01812`: `powershell.exe -NoP -NonI -W Hidden -enc <base64>` run by `powershell.exe`.\nLet's decode the base64 command line in `evt-01812` or let's look at what is in registry writing events (Sysmon Event ID 12 or 13 or similar) and script blocks.",
  "plan": "I will decode the base64 string from `evt-01812` to see what PowerShell script was run. Also, I'll examine the script block logging (event_code = 4104) and any registry events (event_code 12 or 13) associated with `HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows Update` or `fodhelper` (ms-settings).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '' | base64 -d | iconv -f UTF-16LE -t UTF-8\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sqlite3 /root/data/eventlogs.db \"SELECT event_id, timestamp, host, event_code, target_object, details FROM events WHERE event_code IN (12, 13, 14) AND (target_object LIKE '%Windows Update%' OR target_object LIKE '%ms-settings%' OR target_object LIKE '%shell%') ORDER BY timestamp;\"\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}
